				\begin{document}$$U = \frac{{n\left( {n + 1} \right)}}{2}{-} \, \sum_{ranks}$$\end{document}2\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$z = \frac{{U - \sigma_{U} }}{{x_{U} }}$$\end{document}where n is the number of items in the samples, ∑ranks is the sum of ranks in the sample, σU is the standard deviation of U, and xU is the mean of U.

The analyses also included the calculation of trophic indices, which determine the content of phosphorus and/or nitrogen compounds, which are the main cause of eutrophication in water bodies. They concerned the calculation of two indices: Carlson (TSIP) and Trophic Level Index (TLI), which are based on the average content of total phosphorus45 (TP, mg/m3) and/or total nitrogen (TN, mg/m3) for the analyzed research period in all research points. In the case of both parameters, transparency (Secchi disk depth) and chlorophyll46 a could also be taken into account, however, due to the small data (N = 22 and 291, respectively), these parameters were omitted, because the results could be unrepresentative. To determine the indices, the following formulas were used:3\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
